{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Any man who shall draw near.</b> This 'drawing near' is nothing but eating. And so we find that the warning against eating sacred offerings in impurity is stated in the language of touching — ״בְּכָל קֹדֶשׁ לֹא תִגָּע״ (Leviticus 12) — a warning to the eater, and our Sages derived it from a gezerah shavah. But should you say he is liable for touching, behold, karet is stated for eating in ״צַו אֶת אַהֲרֹן״ (Leviticus 7) — two karet-statements side by side, and if he is liable for touching, there was no need to make him liable for eating. And so it is expounded in Torat Kohanim: 'But is one who touches liable? If so, what does Scripture say יִקְרַב? From when he is fit to draw near — for they are not liable on account of impurity unless those who permit it have drawn near' (Sifra; Zevaḥim 45). And if you say: three karet-statements for priestly impurity — why? They have already been expounded in tractate Shevuot (7a) — one for the general rule and one for the specific case, etc." "<b>And his impurity is upon him.</b> The impurity of the person is upon him — one might think Scripture speaks of the flesh: the impurity of the flesh is upon it. And in a clean person who ate the unclean — Scripture speaks; perforce you learn from its sense: Scripture speaks of one whose impurity departs from him of itself, and this is a person who has purification by immersion (Sifra)." "<b>She shall be cut off.</b> One might think from this side to that side — cut off from his place and settle in another place. Scripture says <b>I am YHWH</b> — in every place, I am (ibid.)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {1 "The argument: if the verse concerned impurity of the flesh of the offering, it would read 'its impurity is upon it'; the masculine suffix עליו combined with אָדָם drives the reading toward the person, not the meat."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:46.249053476Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}